            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: what did āmēle mean?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: ಕೂಡ",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ಕೂಡ (kūḍa) — \"also, too\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ಕೂಡ (kūḍa) goes after the word it adds, not in front of it: ನಾನು ಕೂಡ (kūḍa), me as well. English puts also almost anywhere in a sentence; Kannada puts this one straight behind the thing it is including and leaves it there."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The same shape carries the idea of coming together, which is where the adding sense comes from — two things joined, and the second one mentioned."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Take care with the middle letter. ಕೂಡ (kūḍa) and ಕೂದಲು from the body chapter open alike and then part company, and in a rough spelling they can look closer than they are."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
